<Base title="About" description="What nuclear.academy is, who writes it, and what it is not.">
  <div class="kicker">About</div>
  <h1>What this site is, and is not</h1>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>nuclear.academy is an open course of study in nuclear security: how the world keeps nuclear material where it belongs, detects activity outside the bounds of appropriate use, and responds when something goes wrong. It is written for readers who follow the subject and want the mechanism rather than the headline — the technically or policy-engaged public first, and students and practitioners alongside them.</p>
    <h2>Who writes it</h2>
    <p>Howard L. Hall is Professor of Nuclear Engineering at the University of Tennessee, Knoxville, and founder of its Institute for Nuclear Security. He came to the university in 2009 after two decades at Lawrence Livermore National Laboratory.</p>
    <h2>What it is not</h2>
    <p>It is not news, not advocacy, and not a guide to anything sensitive. Everything here is built from the open literature — IAEA guidance, U.S. regulations, peer-reviewed work, public agency documents — and says where it came from. Durable modules are kept apart from dated situation pages. Nothing you do here is tracked.</p>
    <p class="meta"><em>Affiliation statement and acknowledgements to be settled by the editor before first release.</em></p>
  </div>
</Base>
